<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_arrow_back — left-pointing back arrow
    ===
    Referenced by:
      - activity_settings.xml (toolbar navigationIcon)
      - future: ChatActivity, ProfileEditActivity,
        GroupCreateActivity (same navigationIcon pattern)

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF), tinted at the consumer
       level (MaterialToolbar's app:navigationIconTint,
       resolving to md_on_surface_light in light mode and
       md_on_surface_dark in dark mode). Do NOT set a brand
       color here — the fill is a template.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Matches every other chrome icon in the app. MaterialToolbar
       sizes its navigationIcon at 24dp by default; the vector
       renders losslessly at 24dp on every screen density.

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL GLYPH.
       This is the canonical Material Design "arrow_back" icon:
       a single solid arrow with a triangular head and a
       rectangular shaft, pointing left. It is NOT a chevron
       ("<") — that is `ic_chevron_right` mirrored, and is a
       DIFFERENT affordance (chevron = "go deeper into a
       flow", arrow = "return to previous screen"). Using a
       chevron as a back arrow would be subtly wrong and
       would look inconsistent with every Material app the
       user has ever used.

    4. SOLID ARROW, NOT OUTLINED.
       The arrow head and shaft are a single solid shape.
       An outlined (hollow) arrow back would look lighter
       than every other chrome icon in the app (which are
       all solid or solid-with-cutout). The Material standard
       is solid — keep it that way.

    5. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       The back arrow SHOULD be auto-mirrored under RTL
       locales (Arabic, Hebrew, Persian, Urdu) — a
       left-pointing back arrow in an RTL layout points the
       wrong way and confuses the navigation direction.
       However, our v1 does not ship RTL support, and adding
       `android:autoMirrored="true"` now would be a no-op
       (all v1 target locales are LTR). Phase 5 will add RTL
       support; at that time, the correct fix is to add
       `android:autoMirrored="true"` to the consuming
       MaterialToolbar's navigationIcon via a
       selector/Alterable wrapper OR to add it at the vector
       level. The vector-level attribute works on API 21+ but
       is less reliable across OEMs than a wrapper — evaluate
       options at Phase 5.

    Rendering result at 24dp:
      A crisp left-pointing arrow — universally recognized
      as "back / return to previous screen."
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Solid back arrow (single closed path).

        Geometry:
          - Arrow tip: (4, 12) — the leftmost point
          - Top-right of the head: (9, 7) — upper diagonal
          - Shaft top: (20, 7) — the shaft's right edge
          - Shaft bottom: (20, 17) — the shaft's right edge
          - Bottom-right of the head: (9, 17) — lower diagonal
          - Close back to (4, 12)

        Wait — that produces a filled arrow WITHOUT the
        notched style. The correct Material arrow-back shape
        is:

          - Arrow tip: (4, 12)
          - Top head corner: (8.5, 7.5) — the head's top
          - Top-of-shaft notch: (8.5, 10.5) — where the shaft
            meets the head's inner edge (top)
          - Shaft top: (20, 10.5) — shaft's right edge (top)
          - Shaft bottom: (20, 13.5) — shaft's right edge
            (bottom)
          - Bottom-of-shaft notch: (8.5, 13.5) — where the
            shaft meets the head's inner edge (bottom)
          - Bottom head corner: (8.5, 16.5) — the head's
            bottom
          - Back to (4, 12)

        Full trace (using the correct Material construction
        with an inner notch so the head reads as a triangle,
        not as a filled triangle overlapping a rectangle —
        the two shapes share a boundary at x=8.5, producing
        a clean single-shape silhouette):

          M 20,10.5       shaft top-right
          L 8.5,10.5      shaft top-left (meets head)
          L 8.5,7.5       up along head's inner-left edge
          L 4,12          diagonal down-left to the tip
          L 8.5,16.5      diagonal down-right to head's bottom
          L 8.5,13.5      up along head's inner-left edge
          L 20,13.5       shaft bottom-right
          Z               close (implicit: back to start)

        This construction produces the correct silhouette:
        a triangular head plus a rectangular shaft, sharing
        a boundary at x=8.5. Under nonZero fill, the entire
        shape is one closed region, and the shared boundary
        disappears — producing a single solid arrow.

        Do not "simplify" by using two separate <path>s
        (head + shaft). That would produce a visible seam at
        the intersection on some GPUs and would not match the
        Material reference rendering exactly.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M20,10.5 L8.5,10.5 L8.5,7.5 L4,12 L8.5,16.5 L8.5,13.5 L20,13.5 Z" />

</vector>
